fication\n\nEctopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic (Corpus Luteum) Ovarian Cyst:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever\n\nEctopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Secondary Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, breast tenderness, collapse, diarrhea, fainting\n  Favours second: chills, fever, foul-smelling vaginal discharge, lower abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting\n\nRuptured Hemorrhagic (Corpus Luteum) Ovarian Cyst vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Secondary Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nRelated to Acute Obstructive Biliary Disease: acute bronchiolitis, acute cervicitis, acute cor pulmonale\n\nRelated to Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum: abdominal ectopic pregnancy, female reproductive system disease, ovarian ectopic pregnancy\n\nRelated to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Secondary Hemoperitoneum: abdominal and pelvic region disorder, acute female pelvic peritonitis, female reproductive system disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → anorexia → Acute Obstructive Biliary Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Acute Obstructive Biliary Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → pain → Acute Obstructive Biliary Disease"}
